{"ts": 1789721323.220577, "kind": "assistant_text", "text": "I submitted feature 8 long, feature 34 short and feature 31 long, with a predicted out-of-sample Sharpe of 0.3 and a standard deviation of 0.4. That combination's in-sample Sharpe is 0.925, but I think most of that is selection luck, which is why the prediction is so much lower.\n\n- **Single features look like noise.** I evaluated all 40 features on their own. Their Sharpes spread with a standard deviation of about 0.21, which matches the roughly 0.22 sampling error you would expect from 5,000 days (about 20 years) if no feature had any real signal.\n- **The three picked are the largest outliers.** Feature 8 scored 0.625, feature 34 scored -0.541 and feature 31 scored 0.434. The largest of 40 pure-noise draws would typically sit around 0.56, so even feature 8 is only marginally convincing.\n- **They combine cleanly.** The pairwise Sharpes of 0.69 to 0.83 are what three uncorrelated signals would give, so the 0.925 for all three is consistent with that.\n- **How I got to 0.3.** I assumed a few percent of features carry a real Sharpe of about 0.5 (the prior is my own guess, not estimated from the data). That gives feature 8 about a two-in-three chance of being real, feature 34 about one in two and feature 31 about one in four, for an expected combined Sharpe of roughly 0.36. I rounded down to 0.3 because the three were picked from 45 evaluations. The 0.4 standard deviation covers both doubt about the true signal and out-of-sample sampling error.\n\nFeatures 8 and 34 alone, or feature 8 by itself, had about the same expected value; I chose three for diversification.\n\nThe Gmail and Google Calendar connectors are not authorized in this session. I did not need them here, but they stay unavailable until you authorize them in your claude.ai connector settings."}
